 brooks was fleeing from a fight  but can't explain why he didn't stop we  have information that the suspect prior  to the incident was involved in a  domestic disturbance  which was just minutes prior  and the suspect left that scene just  prior to our arrival  to the domestic disturbance brooks was  arrested shortly after and faces at  least five counts of intentional  homicide  as the investigation continues this town  is left to clean up and grieve the  tragedy happened just days before the  u.s thanksgiving holiday  so many families who are preparing to  gather and celebrate are instead left to  deal with unthinkable loss jackson  proskow global news washington  ahead buyer beware the historic supply  chain crunch affecting your grocery cart  a significant hearing is underway at the  crtc over a planned merger that could  alter canada's telecom industry a  five-day canadian radio television and  telecommunications commission hearing is  focused on the bid by rogers  communications to take over shaw  communications the companies announced  the 26 billion deal last march today  edward rogers who has been at the center  of a family battle for control of rogers  made his case for the merger  we believe passionately that a  combination of rogers and shaw will help  contribute to a stronger facility-based  future for canadians  this is the first step in the approvals  process the rogers takeover of shaw must  get approval from the crtc the  competition bureau of canada and the  federal department of innovation science  and economic development  a major supply chain crunch is affecting  almost every industry these days driven  by the pandemic and by the catastrophic  weather events in bc that cut off or  damage supply lines companies really  feeling the pinch are grocery stores and  gaviola reports on the impact on  canadian food producers and your  household budget  the supply chain crunch is forcing big  changes at canada's largest distribution  hub for fruits and vegetables we're  flying product in more than we would  have in the past obviously it's more  expensive but we're also starting to  bring product in a little bit earlier in  some cases than we maybe would have to  understand what's happening here's an  example of a shipment of blueberries  coming to canada from peru pre-pandemic  the journey would take 10 to 12 days but  labor strikes in peru and short staffed  ports can add five days with west coast  ports jammed many ships are diverting  east and those blueberries are getting  stuck in a traffic backlog adding  another five days u.s labor shortages  and coveted protocols are causing  inspection delays and goods left sitting  in shipping containers that's another  two to three days and finally the  shortage of truck drivers can bring that  journey to 25 days but the shelf life of  a fresh refrigerated blueberry is just a  week or two so produce likely won't last  as long by the time it reaches you  climate change is a supply chain issue  this food economist says the increased  frequency and severity of extreme  weather events are having profound  impacts on supply now in bc panic buying  that left shelves empty is temporary but  the disruption to dozens of farms will  have a longer lasting ripple effect  drought conditions in the u.s southwest  will also increase prices on items we  have to import this winter like  strawberries broccoli and brussels  sprouts and record-breaking summer  temperatures are now translating into a  spike between 5 and 25 percent in beef  prices this coming year with the cost of  feeding caring for and shipping cattle  rising expect to pay even more for food  though widespread shortages aren't  likely i don't think that we are in any  great danger of seeing empty shelves  in canada we have this  large and complex and integrated food  supply chain solomon zoo runs a small  toronto grocery shop he says the impact  behind the scenes favors his larger  rivals when supply is very low they tend  to give the produce to higher stores  like the chain stores instead of the  small businesses so then we have  less stuff to sell it's an added toll on  household budgets that can't afford to  stretch ann gaviola global news toronto  next making the case to award the  highest military honor to a veteran of  the war in afghanistan  canada's highest military honor is the  victoria cross and it has not been  awarded since the second world war  there's a big push to change that and  grant private jess la rochelle an honor  many veterans think is long overdue he  stood his ground against the taliban in  the face of impossible odds as mike  truly reports the campaign to award him  the victoria cross has now caught the  eye of canada's top soldier  the private jess larochelle never really  stood out amongst the men of 33 bravo he  was quiet and unassuming a good soldier  but heroic  it might not have been known when this  picture was taken  but it certainly is now if you compare  jess larichelle's valorous actions on  the 14th of october 2006 they measure up  with anybody's who has been awarded the  victoria cross  it was 2pm when the call came in  contact imminent la rochelle who was  manning an observation post by himself  was knocked unconscious by an rpg his  back was broken his retina detached and  eardrum blown  and when he came to he alone stood  between roughly 30 taliban and what was  left of his battalion using m 72 rockets  he drove the taliban back  [Music]  it wasn't until after he'd helped carry  his friend's caskets at the next day's  ramp ceremony that he got checked out at  the hospital  la rochelle was awarded the star of  military valor yet now 15 years later a  group of veterans and historians have  launched a petition to right a wrong and  see the victoria cross pinned to his  chest i think the problem is because we  haven't given one out since world war ii  we have set the bar  so high  that basically it's like not allowing  anybody into the hockey hall of fame  unless you're better than wayne gretzky  to reopen the victoria cross  conversation canada's awards and honors  committee needs direction from a senior  government official  and it may now have it a spokesperson  for general wayne eyre says the chief of  defense staff is consulting with  commonwealth allies and reviewing  information specific to the request to  reopen the file of private larochel  wayne is going to look for the best  possible the right solution out of this  and he wants to consider everything  that's happened i know  and i support him in doing exactly that  whether it's humility or the lingering  ptsd larichel is reticent to relive the  chaos of that day and it still takes  everything he's got to manage the  physical impacts of his injuries  he fought and saved lives it's only  right that others now fight for him  mike drolight global news toronto  and that is global national for this  monday i'm donna friesen tonight's your  canada is waves crashing into the rocks  at torbay newfoundland and labrador we'd  love to see your canada please email it  to viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching robin gill will be  here at the anchor desk tomorrow and i  will see you again on thursday take care  of yourselves  bye-bye  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

